.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Key revocation lists, in the binary format of OpenSSH (PROTOCOL.krl, made by ssh\-keygen \-k and used by sshd's RevokedKeys).
.PP
The application reads the file (this library does no I/O), loads its bytes, and asks of each key it is about to trust whether the list revokes it:
.IP \(bu 3
a key listed in full, or by the SHA\-1 or SHA\-256 hash of its blob (a certificate by the key it certifies, and a certificate is also revoked if its CA key is listed so);
.IP \(bu 3
a certificate, by its serial number or key ID, for the CA that signed it or, in a list for no CA in particular, for any.  Serial 0 is never matched, as no CA that does not number its certificates can mean one by it.
.PP
The authentication machine does this for the keys of users if its configuration has a list (gf_auth_server_config).  A client checks the host key in its host_key callback.
.PP
A list is refused whole if it is malformed, has a section this library does not know, a critical extension, a serial number of 0, a range that runs backwards or a bitmap that runs past 2^64: a revocation that is not understood is never ignored.  So is a list with signature sections, as OpenSSH 9.4 and later do (its authors recommend SSHSIG signatures over the file instead, for a list fetched over an untrusted channel; nothing here verifies where a list came from).
.PP
The hash of SHA\-1 sections is the third use of SHA\-1 in the library (after the hashed names of known_hosts and the OCSP responder names): it only recognises a key in files that ssh\-keygen \-k writes, and could at worst be made to revoke a key that was not meant to be.
.PP
Threading: a list is not changed by the check function, so it may be shared by readers while nobody loads.
